# Pictures that move: a flip book and a cartoon as still drawings shown fast, drawings counted for
# seconds on ones and twos, a missing page, in-betweens counted, and a flip book made by hand.
lesson art-pictures-that-move v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=7 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Pictures that move: a flip book"
  goal "Plan an animation by its drawings: how many for a second, what goes in between, and why it moves."

  look {
    scene 21x8 {
      moonphases m from=0 step=4 count=6 blank=-1 days=0 at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      A flip book is a pad of pictures, each a little different from the last. Flip the pages fast
      and the pictures seem to move. John Barnes Linnett patented one in 1868 and called it a
      kineograph, a moving picture. A cartoon film works the same way: since about 1930, films have
      shown 24 frames a second, and animators often draw on twos, showing each drawing for two
      frames, so twelve drawings make a second. An animator draws the first and last pictures of a
      movement, then the in-betweens.
      """
  }
  do {
    show art.flip-drawings t=2 o=0 easy-t=2 hard-t=3 hard-o=1
    show art.flip-missing s=1 easy-s=1 hard-s=5
    level easy {
      worked art.flip-time p=24 level=medium
    }
    show art.flip-time p=36 hard-p=48
    show art.flip-between a=2 b=14 g=3 hard-g=2
    show art.flip-smooth r=0
    show art.flip-drawings t=4 o=1 easy-t=3 easy-o=0 hard-t=5
    level medium hard {
      show art.flip-missing s=5 hard-s=12
    }
    show art.flip-between a=3 b=15 g=2 easy-g=3 hard-g=1
    level medium hard {
      show art.flip-time p=48 hard-p=60
    }
    level easy {
      show art.flip-smooth r=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.flip-why r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.flip-make v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Still pictures shown quickly look like one moving picture: the smaller the change from page to page, the smoother it moves."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that animation is still pictures shown quickly, about twelve or more a second, so it
    is planned by counting drawings and by making each change small and even. The wrong ideas to
    watch for are mixing up drawings on ones and on twos, and counting the steps between the first
    and last drawings as the in-betweens, which is one too many. In the working, look for the child
    listing the heights or the pages before counting them. The core counts drawings for seconds of
    film, fills a missing page of the moon, finds how long a flip book lasts, chooses the smoother
    book and counts in-betweens; the two-star asks why the pictures seem to move, and the three-star
    is a flip book of the child's own, which you look at with the notice list rather than mark.

    The facts are from Wikipedia: "Flip book" for Linnett's kineograph, patented on 18 March 1868;
    "Frame rate" for 24 frames a second, standard for sound film from 1927 to 1930, for animating on
    twos at 12 drawings a second, and for the eye seeing 10 to 12 pictures a second one by one and
    more as motion. If the in-betweens are stuck, write the heights in a row and ring the first and
    last. Off the page, make a two-page flip book: draw a face on one sheet and the same face
    winking on the sheet under it, then roll the top sheet round a pencil and flick it. China's 2022
    arts standard asks for animation and moving pictures among the media children of grades 3 to 5
    make with (recalled, not fetched); this lesson meets England's key stage 2 aim that pupils
    "improve their mastery of art and design techniques".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the drawings are counted on twos, the missing page is between a crescent and a half moon, how long a flip book lasts is worked once in the teacher's pen before one of 36 pages, the in-betweens are 3 cm apart, both smoothness questions are asked, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the flip book has 12 pages of a rolling ball."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the drawings are counted on ones, the missing pages are later in the month, the flip books are 48 and 60 pages, the in-betweens are 2 cm and 1 cm apart, and the flip book is a bouncing ball that slows near the top."
  }
}
